WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.766
Many modern C++ lifetime defects involve non-owning views and temporary objects. std::string_view and std::span are excellent tools when the

2
00:00:07.766 --> 00:00:09.531
underlying storage outlives the view.

3
00:00:09.531 --> 00:00:16.591
They are dangerous when the view is stored or returned without a clear guarantee that the referenced data remains alive.

4
00:00:16.591 --> 00:00:19.415
A view should be understood as borrowed access.

5
00:00:19.415 --> 00:00:23.298
It does not own the characters, elements, or bytes it describes.

6
00:00:23.298 --> 00:00:32.476
If the source is a temporary string, a local vector, a transformed buffer, or data inside a container that may later reallocate, the view can outlive

7
00:00:32.476 --> 00:00:34.947
the data unless the design prevents it.

8
00:00:34.947 --> 00:00:38.124
References to temporaries and returned references require close review.

9
00:00:38.124 --> 00:00:44.125
Some C++ lifetime extension rules are helpful, but they do not make every reference safe to store.

10
00:00:44.125 --> 00:00:51.538
A function should not return a reference, pointer, span, or string view to data whose lifetime ends with the function call.

11
00:00:51.538 --> 00:00:56.480
Callbacks, lambdas, and asynchronous work make lifetime reasoning harder because execution may happen later.

12
00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:03.893
Capturing a local variable by reference is safe only while the referenced object remains alive and the callback cannot run afterward.

13
00:01:03.893 --> 00:01:07.423
Deferred execution should prefer owned captures or explicit lifetime coordination.

14
00:01:07.423 --> 00:01:15.895
When lifetime is uncertain, prefer owned data, shared ownership when truly required, weak observation with a check before use, or a design that makes

15
00:01:15.895 --> 00:01:16.601
coordination visible.

16
00:01:16.601 --> 00:01:19.778
Borrowed views are safest when used briefly and locally.

17
00:01:19.778 --> 00:01:24.720
The longer a borrowed view is stored, the stronger its contract needs to be.
